hadiths[270]{hadithnumber,text}:
"205","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), that he said: ""I command you with three and forbid you from three. I command you: to worship Allah and not associate anything with Him, to hold firmly to the rope of Allah all together and not to divide, and to obey whoever Allah appoints over your affairs. And I forbid you from prattle, excessive questioning, and wasting wealth."
"206","On the authority of Jabir ibn Sulaym (or Salim), who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found him sitting with his companions. I said, ""Which of you is the Prophet (peace be upon him)?"" He said: Either he gestured to himself, or the people gestured to him. He said: And there he was, wearing a cloak (burdah) with its hem falling on his feet. I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I am ignorant about many things, so teach me."" He said: ""Fear Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and do not belittle any good deed, even if it is pouring from your bucket into the vessel of one who asks for water. And beware of arrogance, for Allah, Blessed and Exalted, does not love arrogance. And if a man insults you and reproaches you with something he knows about you, do not reproach him with something you know about him; then you will have its reward and he will bear its sin. And do not insult anyone."""
"207","[AI-translation] From Umm Salama that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to say in his illness: Fear Allah regarding the prayer and what your right hands possess. And he kept repeating them."
"208","[AI-translation] From Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, marfu'an: ""The most beloved food to Allah is that over which many hands partake."
"209","On the authority of Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, which people are most beloved to Allah? And which deeds are most beloved to Allah?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The most beloved of people to Allah the Exalted is the one who is most beneficial to people. And the most beloved of deeds to Allah the Mighty and Majestic is bringing joy to a Muslim, or relieving him of a distress, or paying off his debt, or dispelling his hunger. And for me to walk with a brother in regard to a need is more beloved to me than secluding myself in this mosque—meaning the Mosque of Medina—for a month. And whoever restrains his anger, Allah will cover his faults. And whoever suppresses his rage, even though he could act upon it if he wished, Allah will fill his heart with hope on the Day of Resurrection. And whoever walks with his brother regarding a need until it is fulfilled for him, Allah will make his footing firm on the Day when feet will slip. (And indeed, bad character spoils deeds just as vinegar spoils honey)."""
"210","[AI-translation] From Yazid ibn Asid that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said to him: Love for people what you love for yourself."
"211","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) heard a word and it pleased him, so he said: We have taken your speech from your mouth."
"212","[AI-translation] From a man from Banu 'Amir that he sought permission to enter upon the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) while he was in a room, and he said: May I enter? The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) said: Go out to this man and teach him how to seek permission, and say to him: Say, ""Peace be upon you, may I enter?"" The man heard him and said: Peace be upon you, may I enter? So the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) gave him permission, and he entered."
"213","[AI-translation] From a man from Banu 'Amir that he sought permission to enter upon the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) and said: May I enter? The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) said to his servant: Go out to him, for he does not know how to seek permission well, and say: Let him say, ""Peace be upon you, may I enter?"
"214","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family): The most despicable name in the sight of Allah on the Day of Resurrection is a man who names himself King of Kings."
"215","[AI-translation] Narrated from 'Abdullah bin Buraidah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: If you send a messenger, send one who is handsome and has a good name."
"216","[AI-translation] Dahhak bin Qais Al-Fihri, who says: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, saying: If a man comes to a people and they say, ""Welcome,"" then welcome to him on the day he meets his Lord. And if a man comes to a people and they say to him, ""Drought,"" then drought for him on the Day of Resurrection."
"217","[AI-translation] Dahhak bin Qais Al-Fihri, who says: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, saying: If a man comes to a people and they say, ""Welcome,"" then welcome to him on the day he meets his Lord. And if a man comes to a people and they say to him, ""Drought,"" then drought for him on the Day of Resurrection."
"218","[AI-translation] He, peace be upon him, said: If the noble one of a people comes to you, then honor him. Narrated from the hadith of 'Abdullah bin 'Umar, Jarir bin 'Abdullah, Jabir bin 'Abdullah, Abu Hurairah, 'Abdullah bin 'Abbas, Mu'adh bin Jabal, 'Adi bin Hatim, Abu Rashid 'Abdur-Rahman bin 'Abd, and Anas bin Malik, may Allah be pleased with them."
"219","[AI-translation] Miqdam bin Ma'di Karib, elevated to the Prophet: If one of you loves his brother, let him inform him that he loves him."
"220","[AI-translation] Narrated from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, elevated to the Prophet: If one of you loves his companion, let him go to him in his home and inform him that he loves him for the sake of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"221","[AI-translation] Narrated from Mujahid, who said: A man from among the Companions of the Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, met me and grabbed my shoulder from behind. He said: ""Verily, I love you."" I said: ""May the One for whom you love me love you."" He said: ""Were it not that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, said: 'If a man loves a man, let him inform him that he loves him,' I would not have informed you."" He said: Then he began to offer me a marriage proposal, saying: ""Verily, we have a slave-girl; verily, she is one-eyed."
"222","[AI-translation] Narrated from 'Abdullah bin Mas'ud, who said: If any one of you wishes to ask, let him begin with praise and extolling Allah with what He is worthy of, then let him send blessings upon the Prophet, peace be upon him, then let him ask after that, for it is more likely to be successful. Stopped in its chain but taking the ruling of an elevated hadith."
"223","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri said: I was sitting in a gathering of the Ansar when Abu Musa came in, looking as if he were frightened. He said: ""I sought permission to enter upon Umar three times, but permission was not granted to me, so I went back."" Umar said: ""What prevented you?"" He replied: ""I sought permission three times, but permission was not granted to me, so I went back, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'If any of you seeks permission three times and permission is not granted to him, let him go back.'"" Umar said: ""By Allah, you must bring proof of this, or has anyone among you heard this from the Prophet ﷺ?"" Ubayy ibn Ka'b said: ""By Allah, no one will stand with you except the youngest of the people."" I was the youngest of the people, so I stood with him and informed Umar that the Prophet ﷺ had said that."
"224","[AI-translation] Narrated from Jabir, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, said: When one of you lies on his back, he should not place one of his legs over the other."
"225","[AI-translation] Darda', elevated to the Prophet: If two Muslim men accompany each other and a tree, a stone, or mud comes between them, let one of them greet the other with peace, and they should exchange the greeting of peace."
"226","[AI-translation] From Mus'ab ibn Shaybah, from his father, marfu'an: ""When one of you reaches a gathering, if space is made for him, let him sit; otherwise, let him look for the widest place he sees and sit in it."
"227","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, marfu'an: ""When one of you reaches a gathering, let him offer salam. And when he intends to get up, let him offer salam; the first is no more entitled than the last."
"228","[AI-translation] Maqburi who said: I sat by Ibn 'Umar and a man was narrating to him, so I joined them. He struck my chest with his hand and said: ""Did you not know that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, said: 'If two are conversing privately, do not sit with them until you ask their permission'?"
"229","[AI-translation] Khudri, that they both informed him that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, saw a spot of phlegm on the wall of the mosque. He took a pebble and scraped it off, then said: ""When one of you spits, let him not spit in front of himself nor to his right, but let him spit to his left or under his left foot."
"230","[AI-translation] From Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, marfu'an: ""If a man speaks a narration and then looks around, it is a trust."
"231","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, said: ""If one of you sees a dream that pleases him, let him mention it and interpret it. And if one of you sees a dream that displeases him, let him not mention it nor interpret it."
"232","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, said: ""If one of you sees a dream he dislikes, let him change his position and spit to his left three times, and let him ask Allah for its good and seek refuge from its evil."
"233","[AI-translation] From Ibn 'Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""If one of you visits his brother and sits with him, let him not get up until he asks his permission."
"234","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""If one of you enters upon his Muslim brother and he feeds him from his food, let him eat and not ask him about it. And if he gives him to drink from his drink, let him drink and not ask him about it."
"235","[AI-translation] He said, peace be upon him: ""If you see the flatterers, throw dust in their faces."" It is reported from the hadith of al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad, 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, Abu Hurayrah, and 'Ubadah ibn al-Thabit, may Allah be pleased with them."
"236","[AI-translation] Sakuni, then al-Awfi, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, said: When you ask Allah, ask Him with the palms of your hands, and do not ask Him with their backs."
"237","[AI-translation] On the authority of Jabir ibn Abdullah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, say: ""When you hear the barking of dogs at night or the braying of donkeys, seek refuge in Allah, for they see what you do not see. And minimize going out when footfalls have quieted down, for Allah dispatches in His night whatever He wills of His creation. And shut the doors and mention the name of Allah over them, for Satan does not open a shut door upon which the name of Allah has been mentioned. And cover the jars, turn over the vessels, and tie up the water skins."
"238","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurairah, raised [to the Prophet]: When the servant of one of you prepares food and endures its heat and toil, let him invite him to eat with him. If he does not invite him, let him offer him some of it."
"239","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurairah, raised [to the Prophet]: When one of you strikes, let him avoid the face, for Allah created Adam in His image."
"240","Abu Burdah narrated: I entered upon Abu Musa in the house of the daughter of Umm al-Fadl. I sneezed and he did not say ""Yarhamuk Allah"" (may Allah have mercy on you) to me. Then she sneezed and he said ""Yarhamuk Allah"" to her. So I returned to my mother and informed her. When she came to him, she said: ""My son sneezed in your presence and you did not say 'Yarhamuk Allah' to him, but she sneezed and you said 'Yarhamuk Allah' to her."" He said: ""Your son sneezed and did not praise Allah the Exalted, so I did not say 'Yarhamuk Allah' to him. But she sneezed and praised Allah the Exalted, so I said 'Yarhamuk Allah' to her. And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: 'When one of you sneezes and praises Allah, then say ""Yarhamuk Allah"" to him. And if he does not praise Allah the Mighty and Majestic, then do not say ""Yarhamuk Allah"" to him.'"" She said: ""You did well, you did well."""
"241","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, raised [to the Prophet]: When one of you sneezes, his sitting companion should invoke mercy upon him. If he sneezes more than three times, he has a cold, and mercy should not be invoked upon him after that."
"242","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abdullah ibn Buraidah, from his father, raised [to the Prophet]: If a man says to a hypocrite, ""O master,"" he has angered his Lord, the Blessed and Exalted."
"243","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When one of you stands from his seat and then returns to it, he has the most right to it."
"244","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If you say to the people, ""Be quiet,"" while they are speaking, you have spoken in vain against yourself (meaning on Friday)."
"245","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu al-Qasim, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When one of you is in the shade, and the shade recedes from him, so that part of him is in the sun and part of him is in the shade, let him stand."
"246","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, said: When three are gathered together, let no two converse privately excluding the third."
"247","[AI-translation] From Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), marfu': When the edge of the night comes, restrain your children, for the devils spread out at that time. Then when an hour of the evening has passed, release them."
"248","[AI-translation] Abu Sufyan (from Jabir) said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) while he was preaching and said: ""O Messenger of Allah! Last night I saw in a dream as if my neck was struck and my head fell off (and rolled away), so I followed it, took it, and put it back."" (The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, laughed) and said: ""If the devil plays with any of you in his sleep, let him not tell it to the people."
"249","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) who said: When one of you meets his brother, let him greet him with peace. If a tree, a wall, or a rock separates them and then he meets him again, let him greet him with peace again."
"250","On the authority of Abu Tamimah al-Hujaymi, from a man of his people, who said: I sought the Prophet ﷺ but could not find him, so I sat down. Then there was a group of people among whom he was, and I did not recognize him, and he was reconciling between them. When he finished, some of them stood up with him and said: ""O Messenger of Allah!"" When I saw that, I said: ""Upon you be peace, O Messenger of Allah! Upon you be peace, O Messenger of Allah! Upon you be peace, O Messenger of Allah!"" He said: ""Indeed, 'Upon you be peace' is the greeting of the dead."" Then he turned to me and said: ""When a man meets his Muslim brother, let him say: 'Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings.'"" Then the Prophet ﷺ returned the greeting to me and said: ""And upon you and the mercy of Allah, and upon you and the mercy of Allah, and upon you and the mercy of Allah."""
"251","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you meet the polytheists (and in a narration: the People of the Book), do not initiate the greeting of peace with them. And if you meet them on the road, force them to its narrowest part."
"252","[AI-translation] Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""If a group of men passes by a people, and a man gives the greeting of peace on behalf of those who passed to those sitting, and one from among them returns it, it suffices for these and for those."
"253","[AI-translation] Ghifari who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you pass by the Jews, do not greet them with peace. If they greet you with peace, say: ""And upon you."
"254","[AI-translation] From Ibn Abbas, who said: A mouse came and began dragging the wick. The servant girl went to shoo it away, but the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Leave it."" So it brought it and dropped it between the hands of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) upon the mat he was sitting on, and it burned a spot the size of a dirham. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""When you go to sleep, extinguish your lamps, for the devil guides something like this to do such a thing and burn you."
"255","[AI-translation] From Sa'id ibn Zaid, marfu': ""The most egregious usury is the insulting of honor."
"256","[AI-translation] From Kaladah ibn Hanbal who said: Safwan ibn Umayyah sent him with milk, colostrum, and truffles to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) was at the upper part of the valley. He said: I entered upon him without greeting or seeking permission, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Go back and say: 'Peace be upon you, may I enter?'"
"257","[AI-translation] He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek help in fulfilling your needs through concealment, for every possessor of a blessing is envied."
"258","[AI-translation] Narrated Jabir: I heard the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) say during a campaign we were on: ""Take plenty of sandals, for a man remains a rider as long as he wears sandals."
"259","On the authority of ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When we departed from Mecca, the daughter of Ḥamza followed us, calling out, ""O uncle! O uncle!"" I took her by the hand and brought her to Fāṭima (may Allah be pleased with her), saying, ""Here is your cousin's daughter.""When we arrived in Medina, I, Zayd, and Jaʿfar disputed over her. I said: ""I took her, and she is my cousin's daughter."" Zayd said: ""She is my brother's daughter."" Jaʿfar said: ""She is my cousin's daughter, and her maternal aunt is with me.""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to Jaʿfar: ""You resemble me in creation and character."" He said to Zayd: ""You are our brother and our freed slave."" He said to me: ""You are from me and I am from you."" Then he said: ""Give her to her maternal aunt, for the maternal aunt is like a mother.""I said: ""Will you not marry her, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""She is my brother's daughter through breastfeeding."""
"260","[AI-translation] Narrated Mu'awiyah bin Abu Sufyan: The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Intercede and you will be rewarded, for I intend a matter but I delay it so that you may intercede and be rewarded."
"261","[AI-translation] Narrated Abdullah bin Amr: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Worship the Most Merciful, feed the food, and spread peace, you will enter Paradise in peace."
"262","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, elevated to the Prophet (peace be upon him): ""The most incapable of people is one who is incapable of supplication, and the stingiest of people is one who is stingy with the greeting of peace."
"263","On the authority of Abdullah ibn Abbas, who said: Salman al-Farisi narrated his story to me from his own mouth. He said: I was a Persian man from the people of Isfahan, from a village thereof called Jayy. My father was the chief (Dihqan) of his village, and I was the dearest of Allah's creation to him. His love for me did not cease until he confined me in his house, like a slave-girl is confined, tending the fire. I strove hard in the Magian religion until I became the custodian of the fire, kindling it and never allowing it to go out for a single hour. He said: My father had a large estate. One day he was busy with a building project, so he said to me: ""O my son! I am occupied with this building today regarding my estate, so go and inspect it."" He commanded me concerning it with some of what he wanted. I went out intending his estate, but I passed by a church of the Christians. I heard their voices therein as they were praying. I did not know the condition of people due to my father confining me in his house. When I passed by them and heard their voices, I entered upon them to see what they were doing. He said: When I saw them, their prayer amazed me and I became inclined to their affair. I said: ""By Allah, this is better than the religion we are upon."" By Allah, I did not leave them until the sun set, and I neglected my father's estate and did not go to it. I said to them: ""Where is the origin of this religion?"" They said: ""In al-Sham (Syria)."" He said: Then I returned to my father, and he had sent people in search of me, and I had distracted him from all his work. When I came to him, he said: ""O my son! Where were you? Did I not entrust you with what I entrusted?"" I said: ""O my father! I passed by some people praying in a church of theirs, and what I saw of their religion amazed me. By Allah, I did not cease being with them until the sun set."" He said: ""O my son! There is no good in that religion. Your religion and the religion of your forefathers is better than it."" I said: ""Nay, by Allah, it is better than our religion."" He feared for me, so he put fetters on my feet and confined me in his house. I sent word to the Christians, saying: ""When a caravan from al-Sham, Christian merchants, arrives, inform me of them."" A caravan of Christian merchants from al-Sham arrived. They informed me of them. I said to them: ""When they finish their business and intend to return to their land, notify me."" When they intended to return, they informed me. I threw off the iron from my feet and went out with them until I arrived in al-Sham. When I arrived, I said: ""Who is the best of the people of this religion?"" They said: ""The bishop in the church."" I went to him and said: ""I have desired this religion and loved to be with you, serving you in your church, learning from you, and praying with you."" He said: ""Enter."" So I entered with him. He was an evil man. He commanded them to give charity and encouraged them in it, but when they brought things to him, he hoarded them for himself and did not give them to the poor, until he had amassed seven jars of gold and silver. I hated him intensely for what I saw him doing. Then he died. The Christians gathered to bury him. I said to them: ""This was an evil man. He commanded you to give charity and encouraged you in it, but when you brought it to him, he hoarded it for himself and gave nothing of it to the poor."" They said: ""What is your knowledge of that?"" I said: ""I will show you his hoard."" They said: ""Show us."" I showed them its location. They brought out seven jars filled with gold and silver. When they saw them, they said: ""By Allah, we will never bury him."" So they crucified him and then stoned him. Then they brought another man and put him in his place. Salman says: I never saw a man who did not pray the five prayers whom I consider better than him; more ascetic in the world, more desirous of the Hereafter, or more constant in worship night and day than him. I loved him with a love I had not loved anyone before him. I stayed with him for a time. Then death approached him. I said to him: ""O so-and-so! I was with you and loved you with a love I had not loved anyone before you. The decree of Allah has come upon you as you see. To whom do you commend me? And what do you command me?"" He said: ""O my son! By Allah, I know no one today upon what I was upon. The people have perished, altered, and abandoned most of what they were upon, except a man in 'al-Raml' (perhaps referring to a specific location or the wilderness), and he is so-and-so. He is upon what I was upon, so join him."" When he died and was buried, I went to the man of al-Mawsil. I said to him: ""So-and-so commended me to you at his death and informed me that you are upon his affair."" He said to me: ""Stay with me."" So I stayed with him. I found him to be the best man upon the affair of his companion. He did not live long before he died. When death approached him, I said to him: ""O so-and-so! So-and-so commended me to you and commanded me to join you. The decree of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has come upon you as you see. To whom do you commend me? And what do you command me?"" He said: ""O my son! By Allah, I know no man upon the like of what we were upon except a man in Nisibis, and he is so-and-so. Join him."" When he died and was buried, I went to the man of Nisibis. I came to him and informed him of my story and what my companion had commanded me. He said: ""Stay with me."" So I stayed with him. I found him upon the affair of his two companions. I stayed with the best of men. By Allah, he did not live long before death descended upon him. When he was attended, I said to him: ""O so-and-so! So-and-so commended me to so-and-so, then so-and-so commended me to so-and-so, then so-and-so commended me to you. To whom do you commend me? And what do you command me?"" He said: ""O my son! By Allah, we know no one remaining upon our affair whom I command you to come to, except a man in Ammuriyah (Amorium). He is upon the like of what we are upon. If you wish, go to him, for he is upon our affair."" When he died and was buried, I went to the man of Ammuriyah and informed him of my story. He said: ""Stay with me."" So I stayed with a man upon the guidance of his companions and their affair. I acquired wealth until I had cows and sheep. Then the decree of Allah descended upon him. When he was attended, I said to him: ""O so-and-so! I was with so-and-so, then so-and-so commended me to so-and-so, and so-and-so commended me to so-and-so, then so-and-so commended me to you. To whom do you commend me? And what do you command me?"" He said: ""O my son! I know no one who has arisen upon what we were upon among the people whom I command you to come to. But the time of a Prophet has overshadowed you. He is sent with the religion of Ibrahim. He will emerge in the land of the Arabs, migrating to a land between two lava tracts (harrahs), between which are date palms. He has signs that are not hidden: he eats the gift but does not eat charity, and between his shoulders is the Seal of Prophethood. If you can reach that land, then do so."" Then he died and was buried. I stayed in Ammuriyah as long as Allah willed. Then a group of Kalb merchants passed by me. I said to them: ""Will you carry me to the land of the Arabs? I will give you these cows and sheep of mine."" They said: ""Yes."" I gave them to them, and they carried me. When they brought me to Wadi al-Qura, they wronged me and sold me to a Jewish man as a slave. I was with him, and I saw the date palms. I hoped that this was the land my companion had described to me, but I was not certain in myself. While I was with him, a cousin of his from Medina, from Banu Qurayzah, came to him. He bought me from him and took me to Medina. By Allah, it was only when I saw it that I recognized it by my companion's description. I stayed there. Allah sent His Messenger. He stayed in Mecca as long as he stayed. I did not hear any mention of him due to what I was in of the occupation of slavery. Then he migrated to Medina. By Allah, I was in the top of a palm tree belonging to my master, working in it, and my master was sitting. A cousin of his came and stood by him. He said: ""Woe to Banu Qaylah! By Allah, they are now gathered at Quba' around a man who has come to them from Mecca today claiming he is a Prophet."" When I heard it, a shudder seized me until I thought I would fall on my master. I climbed down from the palm tree and began saying to his cousin: ""What is that? What do you say? What do you say?"" My master became angry and punched me a severe blow, saying: ""What have you to do with this? Get on with your work!"" I said: ""Nothing. I only wanted to ascertain what he said."" I had something I had collected. When evening came, I took it and went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Quba'. I entered upon him and said: ""It has reached me that you are a righteous man, and with you are companions who are strangers in need. This is something I had for charity, and I saw you more deserving of it than others."" I brought it near to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his companions: ""Eat,"" and he withheld his hand and did not eat. I said to myself: ""This is one."" Then I turned away from him. I collected something else, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ moved to Medina. Then I came with it and said: ""I saw you do not eat charity, and this is a gift with which I have honored you."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ate from it and commanded his companions, and they ate with him. I said to myself: ""These are two."" Then I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at Baqi' al-Gharqad. He had followed a funeral of one of his companions. He had two cloaks on and was sitting among his companions. I greeted him, then I moved around to look at his back to see if I could see the Seal my companion had described to me. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw me moving around him, he knew I was ascertaining something described to me. He threw his cloak off his back. I looked at the Seal and recognized it. I threw myself upon him, kissing it and weeping. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: ""Turn around."" So I turned around. I narrated my story to him as I have narrated it to you, O Ibn Abbas. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was pleased that his companions should hear that. Then slavery occupied Salman until he missed Badr and Uhud with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: ""Make a contract (for manumission), O Salman."" So I contracted with my master for three hundred date palms to be planted for him by the poor (or: by the Furay' - a specific measure/method), and forty Uqiyahs (of silver). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Help your brother."" So they helped me with the palm shoots: a man with thirty shoots, a man with twenty, a man with fifteen, a man with ten—each man according to what he had—until three hundred shoots were gathered for me. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: ""Go, O Salman! Dig holes for them. When you are finished, come to me so that I may plant them with my own hands."" I dug holes for them, and my companions helped me until I finished. I came to him and informed him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out with me to them. We brought the shoots near to him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ planted them with his hand. By Him in Whose hand is the soul of Salman, not a single shoot died. I fulfilled the palms, but the money (silver) remained due from me. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought a piece of gold the size of a hen's egg from some expedition. He said: ""What did the Persian manumitted slave do?"" I was summoned for him. He said: ""Take this and pay off what is upon you, O Salman."" I said: ""Where does this fall, O Messenger of Allah, compared to what is upon me?"" He said: ""Take it, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic,"""
"264","[AI-translation] Bara', elevated to the Prophet (peace be upon him): ""Spread peace and you will be safe."
"265","[AI-translation] Narrated Ibn Umar, elevated to the Prophet (peace be upon him): ""Spread peace, feed the food, and be brothers as Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has commanded you."
"266","[AI-translation] He (peace be upon him) said: ""Kill the snakes and the dogs, and kill the one with two stripes and the tailless one, for they seek the sight and cause miscarriages."" This is reported from the hadith of Ibn Umar and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with them."
"267","[AI-translation] Narrated Jabir bin Abdullah, elevated to the Prophet (peace be upon him): ""Minimize going out after the footfalls have quieted, for Allah, Exalted be He, has creatures He spreads throughout the earth at that hour."
"268","[AI-translation] Narrated from Hisham bin 'Urwah, from his father, that Aishah said to the Prophet (peace be upon him): ""O Messenger of Allah, all your wives have a kunya except me."" So the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to her: ""Take a kunya from your son Abdullah, meaning Ibn al-Zubayr: you are Umm Abdullah."" He said: She was then called Umm Abdullah until she died, though she never gave birth."
"269","[AI-translation] Narrated from Shaqiq who said: Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, said Labbaik at As-Safa, then said: ""O tongue, speak good and you will profit, remain silent and you will be safe before you regret."" They said: ""O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, is this something you say or something you heard?"" He said: ""No, rather I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) say: 'Most of the sins of the son of Adam are in his tongue.'"
"270","[AI-translation] From Fudalah ibn 'Ubayd who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said during the Farewell Pilgrimage: ""Shall I not tell you who the believer is? He is the one whom people trust with their wealth and their lives. The Muslim is the one from whose tongue and hand the people are safe. The mujahid is the one who strives against himself in obeying Allah. And the muhajir is the one who abandons sins and transgressions."
"271","On the authority of Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) came out to them while they were sitting, and he said: ""Shall I not inform you of the best of people in status?"" We said: ""Yes."" He said: ""A man holding the reins of his horse"" — or he said: ""a horse in the path of Allah until he dies or is killed."" He said: ""Shall I inform you of the one next to him?"" We said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""A man secluded in a mountain pass, establishing prayer and giving zakah, and keeping away from people."" He said: ""Shall I inform you of the worst of people in status?"" We said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""The one who is asked in the name of Allah the Great and does not give."""
"272","[AI-translation] From Ibn Abbas, raised to the Prophet: ""Shall I not inform you of your men among the people of Paradise? The Prophet is in Paradise, the Siddiq is in Paradise, the martyr is in Paradise, the newborn is in Paradise, and the man who visits his brother in the outskirts of the city, visiting him only for Allah, Mighty and Majestic. And your women among the people of Paradise? The loving, child-bearing, devoted to her husband, who, when he is angry, comes until she places her hand in her husband's hand and says, 'I will not taste sleep until you are pleased.'"
"273","[AI-translation] From Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""Beware, a man must not spend the night with a previously married woman unless he is her husband or a mahram."
"274","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah that the Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, said: ""O Allah, I take a covenant with You which You will not break. I am only human, so whichever believer I have harmed, insulted, cursed, or whipped, make it for him a prayer, a charity, and a nearness by which You draw him closer to You on the Day of Resurrection."
"275","[AI-translation] From Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, came to us (visiting in our house) and saw a man with disheveled, scattered hair. He said: ""Could this one not find something to tidy his hair with?"" And he saw another man with dirty clothes, and he said: ""Could this one not find water to wash his clothes with?"
"276","[AI-translation] From Ibn 'Umar, raised to the Prophet: ""Jibril ordered me to advance the elders."
"277","[AI-translation] Aslami who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah! Command me to do a deed I should do."" He said: ""Remove harmful things from the road, for it is a charity for you."
"278","[AI-translation] Juhani who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah! What is salvation?"" He said: ""Restrain your tongue, let your house suffice you, and weep over your sins."
"279","[AI-translation] Muharibi who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, advise me."" He said: ""Control your hand."" And in another narration: ""Do not extend your hand except towards good."
"280","[AI-translation] Bara' who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, passed by an assembly of the Ansar and said: ""If you insist on sitting, then guide the way, return the greeting, and help the oppressed."
"281","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man complained to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him and his family, about the hardness of his heart, so he said to him: If you wish to soften your heart, feed the poor and wipe the head of the orphan."
"282","[AI-translation] From 'Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, marfu'an: Verily, the people with the greatest crime are a poet who satirizes an entire tribe, and a man who disowns his father."
"283","[AI-translation] From 'Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: Verily, the greatest of people in falsehood is a man who satirizes a man, so he satirizes the entire tribe, and a man who disowns his father and accuses his mother of fornication."
"284","[AI-translation] From 'Abdullah bin 'Amr, marfu'an: Verily, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, hates the eloquent among men who wields his tongue like a cow wields its tongue."
"285","[AI-translation] From 'Abdullah bin 'Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him and his family, was asked about the 'Aqiqah? He said: ""Allah does not love 'Uquq."" And it was as if he disliked the name. They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we only ask you: when one of us has a child born to him."" He said: ""Whoever of you wishes to offer a sacrifice on behalf of his child, let him do so. For a boy, two sheep, equal in age, and for a girl, one sheep."
"286","[AI-translation] From Husain bin 'Ali, marfu'an: Verily, Allah loves the lofty matters and their heights, and dislikes their base ones."
"287","[AI-translation] From Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""A dream rests upon what it is interpreted as, and the likeness of that is the likeness of a man who raised his leg, so he is waiting when he will put it down. So if one of you sees a dream, let him not narrate it except to a sincere advisor or a knowledgeable one."
"288","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurairah, marfu'an: A man visited a brother of his in a village, so Allah, the Exalted, stationed an angel on his path. When the angel came upon him, he said: Where do you intend to go? He said: I am visiting a brother of mine in this village. He said: Is there any favor upon him that you are tending to? He said: No, except that I loved him for the sake of Allah. He said: Then I am a messenger from Allah to you, that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has loved you just as you loved him for Him."
"289","[AI-translation] From Jundub that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him and his family, narrated: A man said, ""By Allah, Allah will not forgive so-and-so,"" and Allah said: ""Who is this who takes an oath upon Me that I will not forgive so-and-so? For I have indeed forgiven so-and-so, and rendered your deeds void"" - or as he said."
"290","[AI-translation] Harith Al-Muzani that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: A man may utter a word of Allah's pleasure that he did not think would reach what it reached, so Allah writes for him His pleasure until the day he meets Him. And a man may utter a word of Allah's wrath that he did not think would reach what it reached, so Allah writes for him His wrath until the day he meets Him."
"291","[AI-translation] From Anas who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""Indeed, as-salam is one of the names of Allah, which He placed in the earth, so spread the salam among yourselves."
"292","[AI-translation] From 'Abdullah, marfu': ""Indeed, as-salam is one of the names of Allah, which Allah has placed in the earth, so spread it among you. For when a man gives salam to a group and they return it to him, he has the merit of a degree over them, because he reminded them. And if they do not return it to him, someone better and purer than them will return it to him."
"293","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurairah that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, say: ""Indeed, a servant speaks a word without considering it, by which he slips into the Fire farther than the distance between the East and the West."
"294","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurairah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""Indeed, everything has a master, and the master of the gatherings is facing the Qiblah."
"295","[AI-translation] Yaman, from the Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, he said: ""Indeed, when a believer meets a believer and gives him salam, and takes his hand and shakes it, their sins scatter off them, just as the leaves of a tree scatter."
"296","[AI-translation] Juhani, marfu': ""Indeed, your cursing one another is not a curse against anyone, for you are the children of Adam, and a sa' has not been filled by you. No one has superiority over another except by religion or righteous deed. It is sufficient for a man to be foul-mouthed, obscene, miserly, and cowardly."
"297","[AI-translation] From Ibn 'Abbas that an Arab bedouin came to the Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, and spoke with eloquent speech (and in a narration by Ahmad: he began praising him). The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: ""Indeed, there is magic in eloquence, and indeed, there is wisdom in poetry."
"298","[AI-translation] From Ubayy ibn Ka'b, marfu': ""Indeed, there is wisdom in poetry."
"299","[AI-translation] From Hani' ibn Yazid who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah! Direct me to a deed that will admit me to Paradise. He said: ""Indeed, among the things that necessitate forgiveness are giving salam and good speech."
"300","Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: A Jew entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""As-samu 'alayka, O Muhammad!"" (Death be upon you). The Prophet ﷺ replied: ""Wa 'alayk"" (And upon you). I intended to speak, but I perceived the Prophet's ﷺ dislike for that, so I remained silent. Then another entered and said: ""As-samu 'alayka."" He said: "" 'Alayk"" (Upon you). I intended to speak, but I perceived the Prophet's ﷺ dislike for that. Then a third entered and said: ""As-samu 'alayka."" I could not restrain myself until I said: ""And upon you be death, and the wrath of Allah and His curse, O brothers of apes and pigs! Do you greet the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with that which Allah has not greeted him with?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed, Allah does not love obscenity nor the practice of it."" They said: ""A word, so we returned it upon them. Indeed, the Jews are a people of envy, and they do not envy us over anything as they envy us over the Salam (greeting of peace) and over the Amin."""
"301","[AI-translation] From Anas who said: When death approached Abu Salamah, Umm Salamah said: ""To whom are you entrusting me?"" He said: ""O Allah, You are better for Umm Salamah than Abu Salamah."" When he died, the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, proposed to her. She said: ""I am advanced in age."" He said: ""I am more advanced in age than you. The dependents are upon Allah and His Messenger, and as for jealousy, I hope Allah will remove it."" So the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, married her, and he sent to her two hand-mills and a water jar."
"302","[AI-translation] From Abu Umamah, marfu': I guarantee a house in the outskirts of Paradise for the one who leaves argumentation even if he is right, and a house in the middle of Paradise for the one who leaves lying even if he is joking, and a house in the highest part of Paradise for the one who improves his character."
"303","[AI-translation] From Jabir bin Sakhr, he said: I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: ""Indeed, we have been forbidden to expose our 'awrah."
"304","[AI-translation] From Sahl bin Sa'd, marfu': I and the sponsor of an orphan are like these two in Paradise. And he pointed with his index and middle fingers and separated them slightly."
"305","[AI-translation] From Ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) changed the name of Asiyah and said: ""You are Jamilah."
"306","[AI-translation] Musayyab, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: ""What is your name?"" He said: ""Hazn."" He said: ""You are Sahl."" He said: ""No, for Sahl is trampled upon and humiliated."" Sa'id said: ""So I thought that after that, roughness would befall us."
"307","[AI-translation] From Jabir, marfu': Let us set off to Al-Basir who is from Banu Waqif so we may visit him. He said: And he was a blind man."
"308","[AI-translation] Ansari, he said: There was a man among the Ansar called Abu Shu'ayb, and he had a slave who was a butcher. He said: ""Prepare food for me so I may invite the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) as the fifth of five."" So he invited the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) as the fifth of five, and a man followed them. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""You invited us as the fifth of five, and this man has followed us. If you wish, you may give him permission, and if you wish, you may leave him."" He said: ""Rather, I give him permission."
"309","A man followed us who was not with us when we were invited; if you permit him, he may enter. This is reported from the hadith of Abu Mas'ud al-Badri and Jabir ibn Abdullah. This is the wording of Abu Mas'ud al-Badri's hadith: He said: A man named Abu Shu'ayb came to his servant, a butcher, and said: ""Prepare food for me sufficient for five, for I saw hunger in the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)."" So he prepared food, then sent word to the Prophet (peace be upon him) inviting him and his companions who were with him. When the Prophet (peace be upon him) stood up, a man who had not been with them when they were invited followed them. When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) reached the door, he said to the owner of the house... and he mentioned it. He said: ""We have permitted him, so let him enter."""
"310","[AI-translation] From Ishaq bin Sa'id, from his father, he said: Abdullah bin Umar came to Abdullah bin Al-Zubayr and said: ""O Ibn Al-Zubayr, beware of deviating in the Sanctuary of Allah, Blessed and Exalted be He, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'A man from Quraysh will deviate in it whose sins, if weighed against the sins of the Thaqalayn, would outweigh them.' He said: 'So beware lest you be him.'"
"311","[AI-translation] From Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When Al-Hasan was born, I named him Hamzah. And when Al-Husayn was born, I named him after his uncle Ja'far. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) called me and said: ""I have been commanded to change the names of these two."" I said: ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" So he named them Hasan and Husayn."
"312","Ummayma bint Ruqayqa reported that she said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) with a group of women to pledge allegiance to him for Islam. We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we pledge allegiance to you on the condition that we will not associate anything with Allah, nor steal, nor commit adultery, nor kill our children, nor bring forth a slander we fabricate between our hands and feet, nor disobey you in what is right.' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'In what you are able and capable of.' She said: 'We said: Allah and His Messenger are more merciful to us than ourselves. Come, let us pledge allegiance to you, O Messenger of Allah.' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'I do not shake hands with women; my word to a hundred women is like my word to a single woman.'"""
"313","[AI-translation] Bara' bin Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said on the day of Qurayzah to Hassan bin Thabit: ""Satirize the polytheists, for Jibril is with you."
"314","[AI-translation] Narrated from Ka'b ibn Malik, elevated to the Prophet: Satirize them with poetry, for the believer strives with his life and his wealth. By Him in Whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, peace be upon him, it is as if you rain arrows upon them."
"315","[AI-translation] Hujaymi who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, instruct me."" He said: ""I instruct you not to be a frequent curser."
"316","[AI-translation] Narrated Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""Beware of conversing after the stillness of the night, for you do not know what Allah brings forth in His creation."
"317","[AI-translation] Narrated from Anas ibn Malik, elevated to the Prophet: Beware of everything for which an excuse is sought."
"318","[AI-translation] Narrated from Mu'awiyah, elevated to the Prophet: Beware of excessive mutual praise, for it is the slaughter."
"319","[AI-translation] Narrated Adi ibn Hatim, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""The most blessed and most ominous thing about a man is what is between his jaws."
"320","[AI-translation] Narrated Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""Two gates whose punishment is hastened in this world: transgression and severing ties of kinship."
"321","[AI-translation] Narrated Aisha who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah! Eat, may Allah make me a ransom for you, reclining; for it is easier on you."" So he lowered his head until his forehead nearly touched the ground, and said: ""Rather, I eat as a slave eats, and I sit as a slave sits."
"322","[AI-translation] Narrated from Ibn Abbas, elevated to the Prophet: ""Blessing is with your elders."
"323","[AI-translation] Narrated from Abu Dharr, elevated to the Prophet: Your smile in the face of your brother is charity for you; your enjoining good and forbidding evil is charity for you; your guiding a man in the land of misguidance is charity for you; your guiding the man with poor sight is charity for you; your removing a stone, thorn, or bone from the road is charity for you; and your pouring from your bucket into your brother's bucket is charity for you."
"324","[AI-translation] From Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, saw me while I was sitting in the sun, so he said: Move to the shade."
"325","[AI-translation] From Jabir, marfu'an: The greeting of a man with one finger with which he points is the act of the Jews."
"326","[AI-translation] From Anas ibn Malik that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: Deliberation is from Allah, and haste is from Satan."
"327","[AI-translation] A'mash from the Prophet, peace be upon him: Deliberation in everything except in the actions of the Hereafter."
"328","[AI-translation] From Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: Three things are not refused: cushions, oil, and milk."
"329","[AI-translation] From Ibn Umar, marfu'an: Three whom Allah will not look at on the Day of Resurrection: the one undutiful to his parents, the habitual drinker of wine, and the one who reminds others of his favors; and three who will not enter Paradise: the one undutiful to his parents, the dayyouth, and the mannish woman."
"330","[AI-translation] From Ibn Abbas who said: A man left Khaybar and two men followed him, while another followed them saying: Turn back, turn back, until he turned them back. Then he caught up with the first one and said: These two are devils, and I did not cease [pressing] them until I turned them back. When you come to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, convey greetings of peace to him and inform him that we are here collecting our charities, and if it were permissible for him, we would have sent it to him. He said: When the man arrived in Madinah, he informed the Prophet, peace be upon him, and at that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, forbade seclusion."
"331","On the authority of Aisha, she said: I went out on the day of the Trench (Khandaq) following the tracks of the people. She said: I heard the vibration of the earth behind me - meaning: the sound of the earth. She said: I turned around and there was Sa'd ibn Mu'adh with his nephew al-Harith ibn Aws carrying his shield. She said: I sat on the ground and Sa'd passed by wearing a coat of mail of iron whose edges protruded, and I feared for Sa'd's extremities. He passed by while reciting: Wait a little, the camel will catch up to the battle. How excellent is death when the appointed time has come. She said: I stood up and entered a garden where there was a group of Muslims, among them Umar ibn al-Khattab, and among them a man wearing a helmet. Umar said: What brought you here? You are indeed bold! She said: He kept blaming me until I wished the earth would split open for me. She said: The man lifted the helmet from his face and it was Talha ibn Ubaydullah. He said: O Umar! Where is the gathering or flight except to Allah? She said: A man from the polytheists of Quraysh called Ibn al-Ariqah shot an arrow at Sa'd and said: Take this, and I am Ibn al-Ariqah. It hit his vein and severed it. Sa'd invoked Allah saying: O Allah, do not let me die until You cool my eyes regarding Banu Qurayza. She said: They were his allies and clients in the pre-Islamic period. His wound healed, and Allah sent the wind upon the polytheists, and Allah sufficed the believers. Abu Sufyan went to Tihama, Uyaynah ibn Badr went to Najd, and Banu Qurayza fortified themselves. The Messenger of Allah returned to Medina and ordered a tent for Sa'd in the mosque. Gabriel came with dust on his teeth and said: Have you laid down the weapons? By Allah, the angels have not yet laid down the weapons. Go out to Banu Qurayza and fight them. The Messenger of Allah put on his armor and besieged them for twenty-five nights. They said: Descend on the judgment of the Messenger of Allah. They consulted Abu Lubabah who gestured slaughter. They said: We descend on the judgment of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh."
"332","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: Five are from the rights of the Muslim upon the Muslim: returning the greeting, answering the invitation, attending the funeral, visiting the sick, and responding to the sneezer when he praises Allah."
"333","[AI-translation] As-Sahihah number (1832) Sunan Ibn Majah, Book of what has been narrated concerning funerals, Chapter of what has been narrated concerning visiting the sick, number (1425)."
"334","[AI-translation] Rahman ibn Abi Amrah al-Ansari, who said: Abu Sa'id was informed of a funeral among his people, so it was as if he delayed until the people took their seats, then he came. And when the people saw him, they dispersed from him, and some of them stood so he could sit in his seat. He said: Verily, I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, say: ""The best of seats are the widest of them."
"335","[AI-translation] From Aishah, marfu: ""The best of you are the best of you to their families, and when your companion dies, leave him."
"336","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: Umar ibn al-Khattab entered while the Abyssinians were playing in the mosque, so he rebuked them. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""Leave them, O Umar, for they are only the sons of Arfidah."
"337","Ibn Abbas narrated: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) visited a man from the Ansar. When he drew near his house, he heard him speaking inside. When he sought permission and entered, he saw no one. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to him: ""I heard you speaking to someone else?"" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I had gone inside, distressed by people's talk on account of the fever I had, when someone entered upon me. I have never seen a man after you with a more honorable sitting nor better speech than him."" He said: ""That was Gabriel (peace be upon him). And indeed among you are men who, if one of them were to swear by Allah, He would fulfill it."""
"338","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, marfu: ""Defend your honor with your wealth."" They asked: ""O Messenger of Allah, how do we defend our honor with our wealth?"" He replied: ""By giving to the poet and to him whose tongue you fear."
"339","[AI-translation] Hasan, marfu mursal: ""May Allah have mercy on a servant who speaks and gains, or remains silent and is safe."
"340","[AI-translation] Narrated Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah: The Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, permitted falsehood in three things: in war, in reconciling between people, and in a man's speech to his wife. And in a narration: ""and a man's conversation with his wife, and a woman's conversation with her husband."
"341","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, marfu: ""Dreams are of three types: a glad tiding from Allah, the self's talk, and a fright from Satan. So if any of you sees a dream that pleases him, let him narrate it if he wishes; and if he sees something he dislikes, let him not narrate it to anyone, and let him stand and pray."
"342","[AI-translation] Khatmi - who was a governor over Kufa - who said: We came to Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah in his house, and the mu'adhdhin gave the call to prayer. We said to Qays: ""Stand and lead us in prayer."" He said: ""I would not lead a people over whom I am not in charge."" Then a man no less than him, called Abdullah ibn Handhala al-Ghasil, said that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, said: ""A man is most entitled to the forepart of his mount, the forepart of his bed, and to lead the prayer in his own dwelling."" At that, Qays ibn Sad said to a mawla of his: ""O so-and-so, stand and lead them in prayer."
"343","[AI-translation] From Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, marfu: ""Reviling a believer is like hovering over the brink of destruction."
"344","On the authority of Sulaymān ibn Ziyād al-Ḥaḍramī, that ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Juzʾ al-Zubaydī narrated to him that he passed by Ayman with a companion of his, and a group of Quraysh had removed their lower garments and made them into strips with which they were striking one another, while they were naked. ʿAbdullāh said: ""When we passed by them, they said, 'These are monks, so leave them alone.'"" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) came out to them. When they saw him, they scattered. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) returned angry until he entered [his house], and I was behind the chamber, so I heard him saying: ""Glory be to Allah! They neither felt shame before Allah, nor did they cover themselves before the Messenger of Allah."" And Umm Ayman was with him, saying: ""Ask forgiveness for them, O Messenger of Allah."" ʿAbdullāh said: ""The Prophet said: 'By no means shall I ask forgiveness for them.'"""
"345","[AI-translation] Narrated Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A boy was born to a man here, and they said: ""What should we name him?"" The Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, said: ""Name him by the most beloved of names to me, Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib."
"346","[AI-translation] Narrated Abdullah, from the Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, who said: ""Peace (Salam) is one of the names of Allah which He has placed upon the earth, so spread it among you. For when a Muslim man passes by a group and greets them with peace and they return his greeting, he has a degree of virtue over them. But if they do not return his greeting, someone better and purer than them will return it to him."
"347","[AI-translation] Narrated Ibn 'Umar: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""Salutation is before the question. So whoever begins questioning you before the salutation, do not answer him."
"348","[AI-translation] Narrated 'Abdullah bin 'Amr, marfu': ""Poetry is in the position of speech; its good is like the good of speech, and its bad is like the bad of speech."
"349","[AI-translation] Narrated 'Amir bin Sa'd, from his father, marfu': ""Purify your courtyards, for the Jews do not purify their courtyards."
"350","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurairah from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""The eater who is grateful is in the position of the faster who is patient."
"351","[AI-translation] Ash'ari, marfu': ""Charity is incumbent upon every Muslim."" It was said: ""What if he does not find anything?"" He said: ""He should work with his hands, benefit himself, and give charity."" It was said: ""What if he is unable?"" He said: ""He should help the distressed person in need."" It was said: ""What if he is unable?"" He said: ""He should enjoin good or virtue."" He said: ""What if he does not do that?"" He said: ""He should withhold from evil, for that is a charity from him."
"352","On the authority of Abū Dharr, who said, marfūʿan: ""Upon every person, on every day the sun rises, there is a charity due from him upon himself."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah! From where do I give charity when we possess no wealth?"" He said: ""For indeed among the doors of charity are: the takbīr, the tasbīḥ, the taḥmīd, the tahlīl, the istighfār, enjoining good, forbidding evil, removing the thorn from the path of people, the bone, and the stone, guiding the blind, making the deaf and the mute understand until they comprehend, guiding the seeker to a need he has whose location you know, striving with the strength of your legs to the distressed one crying for help, and supporting the weak with the strength of your arms. All of that is from the doors of charity from you upon yourself. And for you in your sexual intercourse with your wife is a reward."" Abū Dharr said: ""How can there be a reward for me in my desire?"" He said: ""Do you see, if you had a child, then he reached maturity and you hoped for his goodness, then he died, would you seek reward for him?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""So you created him?"" He said: ""Rather, Allah created him."" He said: ""So you guided him?"" He said: ""Rather, Allah guided him."" He said: ""So you provided for him?"" He said: ""Rather, Allah was the One providing for him."" He said: ""Likewise, so place it in its lawful place and avoid its unlawful place; for if Allah wills, He will give it life, and if He wills, He will cause it to die, and for you is a reward."""
"353","[AI-translation] Narrated Ibn 'Umar, marfu': ""Hang the whip where the people of the household can see it."
"354","[AI-translation] Narrated Ibn 'Abbas, marfu': ""Hang the whip where the people of the household can see it, for it is a discipline for them."
"355","[AI-translation] Ansari: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, say: ""Cover the vessels and tie the waterskins, for there is a night in the year during which a pestilence descends. It does not pass by any uncovered vessel or untied waterskin except that some of that pestilence falls into it."
"356","[AI-translation] Narrated Wahshi: A man said, ""O Messenger of Allah, we eat but do not become satiated."" He said: ""Perhaps you eat separately. Gather together for your food and mention the name of Allah upon it, so it may be blessed for you therein."
"357","[AI-translation] Narrated Ibn 'Abbas, marfu': ""In the son of Adam are three hundred and sixty joints, or bones, or articulations; charity is incumbent upon each one every day. Every good word is charity, assisting a man's brother is charity, a drink of water you offer is charity, and removing harm from the road is charity."
"358","[AI-translation] From Aishah, from the Prophet (peace be upon him) regarding His saying: ""That is more suitable that you may not incline,"" he said: ""That you do not act unjustly."
"359","[AI-translation] From Aishah, that the Prophet (peace be upon him): whenever he retired to his bed every night, he would join his palms together, then blow into them, then recite in them: ""Say, He is Allah, the One,"" ""Say, I seek refuge in the Lord of the daybreak,"" and ""Say, I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind."" Then he would wipe with them whatever he could of his body, beginning with his head, his face, and the front of his body. He would do this three times."
"360","[AI-translation] From Abu Musa who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), whenever he sent one of his Companions on some errand, would say: ""Give glad tidings and do not repel; make things easy and do not make things difficult."
"361","[AI-translation] From Anas, from the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family): that whenever he spoke a word, he would repeat it three times so that it would be understood from him, and whenever he came upon a people and greeted them with peace, he would greet them three times."
"362","[AI-translation] From Aishah, elevated [to the Prophet]: Whenever he sat in a gathering or prayed a prayer, he would speak certain words. So Aishah asked him about the words, and he said: ""If he had spoken good, it would be a seal upon them until the Day of Resurrection, and if he had spoken other than that, it would be an expiation for him: 'Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise be to You. There is no god but You. I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You.'"
"363","[AI-translation] From Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: Whenever he left his house, he would say: ""In the name of Allah, I rely upon Allah. O Allah, we seek refuge in You from that we stumble"" (and in a narration: I stumble... using the singular in all the verbs) ""or we go astray or are led astray, or we do wrong or are wronged, or we act ignorantly or are treated ignorantly."
"364","[AI-translation] From Anas bin Malik: Whenever he (peace be upon him and his family) shook hands with a man, he would not let go of his hand until he [the man] was the one to let go of the hand of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family)."
"365","[AI-translation] Janahayn: Whenever he (peace be upon him and his family) sneezed, he would praise Allah, so it would be said to him: ""May Allah have mercy on you,"" and he would say: ""May Allah guide you and set right your affairs."
"366","[AI-translation] From Anas, he said: ""The Companions of the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family), whenever they met, would shake hands, and whenever they returned from a journey, they would embrace."
"367","[AI-translation] From Jabir, he said: His Companions would walk in front of him when he went out, leaving his back for the angels."
"368","[AI-translation] Darimi: Two men among the Companions of the Prophet, peace be upon him, when they met, would not part until one of them recited to the other: ""By Al-'Asr. Verily, man is in loss,"" and then one of them would greet the other with peace."
"369","[AI-translation] Narrated Anas: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, was standing in prayer in his house when a man came and peered into the house. So the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, took an arrow from his quiver and aimed it toward his eyes until he withdrew."
"370","[AI-translation] Narrated Anas bin Malik who said: The people of Jahiliyyah had two days in every year in which they would play. When the Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, arrived in Madinah, he said: ""You had two days in which you played, but Allah has replaced them for you with what is better than them: the Day of Fitr and the Day of Adha."
"371","[AI-translation] Narrated Aishah: Some people from the Jews used to visit the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, and say, ""As-Sam (death) be upon you."" He would say, ""And upon you."" Aishah perceived this and cursed them. (In a narration: Aishah said: ""Rather, upon you be As-Sam and Ad-Dam."") The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""Take it easy, O Aishah (do not be foul-mouthed), for Allah does not love obscenity and foul language."" She said: ""I said: O Messenger of Allah, they say such and such."" He said: ""Did I not already reply to them?"" Then Allah, Almighty and Majestic, revealed: ""And when they come to you, they greet you with that with which Allah has not greeted you."
"372","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurayrah: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, used to call the female of the horses a faras."
"373","[AI-translation] Narrated Ubaydullah bin Ali bin Abi Rafi from his grandmother Salma who said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, disliked that food be taken from the top of the dish."
"374","[AI-translation] Narrated Abdullah bin Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, disliked that anyone step on his heel, but rather [to walk] to the right and left."
"375","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurayrah, marfu'an: Every sermon in which there is no tashahhud is like the severed hand."
"376","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurayrah, marfu'an: Every sermon in which there is no tashahhud is like the severed hand."
"377","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: Every soul from the children of Adam is a sayyid; the man is the sayyid of his family, and the woman is the sayyidah of her house."
"378","[AI-translation] Narrated Ibn 'Umar: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Eat together and do not separate, for the food of one suffices for two, and the food of two suffices for four."
"379","[AI-translation] Narrated Jabir bin Samura: When we reached the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family), each of us would sit wherever he arrived."
"380","[AI-translation] Narrated Zaid bin Arqam: When the Prophet (peace be upon him) greeted us with peace, we would say: ""And upon you be peace, Allah's mercy, His blessings, and His forgiveness."
"381","[AI-translation] Narrated Ibn 'Umar: We used to drink while standing and eat while walking during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)."
"382","[AI-translation] He (peace be upon him) said: ""For the belly of one of you to be filled with pus until it consumes him is better for him than to be filled with poetry."
"383","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Mas'ud from the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family): ""The Muslim has four duties towards his fellow Muslim: to invoke Allah's mercy upon him when he sneezes, to answer him when he invites him, to attend his funeral when he dies, and to visit him when he is sick."
"384","[AI-translation] Narrated Anas bin Malik: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""When my Lord (the Mighty and Majestic) took me on the night journey, I passed by a people who had fingernails of copper, with which they were scratching their faces and chests. I said: 'Who are these, O Jibril?' He said: 'These are those who eat the flesh of people and attack their honor.'"
"385","[AI-translation] Narrated Zaid bin Aslam from his father: 'Umar bin Al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) came upon Abu Bakr As-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was sticking out his tongue. He said: ""What are you doing, O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah?"" He replied: ""This is what brought me to ruin. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said: 'There is no part of the body that does not complain to Allah against the tongue for its harshness.'"
"386","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Huraira, marfu': ""The middle of the road is not for women."
"387","[AI-translation] Narrated Ibn 'Abbas: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""He is not a believer who eats his fill while his neighbor beside him is hungry."
"388","[AI-translation] Narrated Abdullah ibn Mas'ud: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: The believer is neither a slanderer, nor a curser, nor an utterer of obscenity, nor a vulgar speaker."
"389","[AI-translation] Rahman ibn Shibl: I heard the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) say: The rider should greet the pedestrian, the pedestrian should greet the one leading an animal, and the fewer should greet the many. Whoever answers the greeting, it is for him, and whoever does not answer, has nothing."
"390","[AI-translation] Shami, raised to the Prophet: The night of the guest is a right upon every Muslim. If he spends the morning in his courtyard, it is a debt upon him; if he wishes, he may exact it, and if he wishes, he may leave it."
"391","[AI-translation] Narrated Aisha: I went to imitate a woman and a man in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: I would not love that I imitated anyone, even if I were to have such and such."
"392","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Umamah, raised to the Prophet: No servant loves another servant for the sake of Allah, Mighty and Sublime be He, except that Allah, Mighty and Sublime be He, honors him."
"393","[AI-translation] Narrated Anas, raised to the Prophet: ""No two men love each other for the sake of Allah, except that the most beloved of them to Allah, Mighty and Sublime be He, is the one whose love for his companion is stronger."
"394","[AI-translation] Narrated Abdullah ibn Amr: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) was never seen eating reclining, nor would two men step on his heel."
"395","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, raised to the Prophet: ""No servant is granted a provision better or more abundant for him than patience."
"396","[AI-translation] Narrated Anas: There was no person whose sight was more beloved to them than the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), yet they would not stand up for him when they saw him, due to what they knew of his dislike for that."
"397","[AI-translation] Narrated Usamah ibn Sharik, raised to the Prophet: ""Whatever you dislike for people to see you doing, do not do it when you are alone."
"398","[AI-translation] Khawlani that Ruh ibn Zinba' visited Tamim al-Dari and found him picking barley for his horse. He said: And his family was around him. He said to him: ""O Ruh, was there not among these anyone to suffice you?"" Tamim said: ""Yes, but I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, say: 'There is no Muslim man who picks barley for his horse and then hangs it upon it, except that a good deed is written for him for every grain.'"
"399","[AI-translation] Bara' ibn 'Azib who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""There are no two Muslims who meet and shake hands with each other except that they are forgiven before they part."
"400","[AI-translation] From Nu'man ibn Bashir, marfu': ""The parable of the believers in their mutual love, mercy, and compassion is like that of the body; if one organ complains of it, the rest of the body responds to it with sleeplessness and fever."
"401","[AI-translation] He, peace be upon him, said: ""Whoever harms the Muslims in their paths, their curse is due upon him."" Narrated from the hadith of Qatadah from Abu Tufayl from Hudhayfah ibn Usayd, marfu'."
"402","[AI-translation] From Jabir, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""Whoever is afflicted with a trial and mentions it, has shown gratitude for it; and whoever conceals it, has been ungrateful for it."
"403","[AI-translation] From Abu Mijlaz, who said: Mu'awiyah entered a house in which were 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr and 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amir. Ibn 'Amir stood up, and Ibn al-Zubayr remained seated—and he was the more experienced of the two. Mu'awiyah said: ""Sit down, O Ibn 'Amir, for I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, say: 'Whoever loves that people should stand up for him, let him take his seat in the Fire.'"
"404","[AI-translation] From Abu Burdah, who said: I arrived in Medina, and 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar came to me and said: ""Do you know why I came to you?"" He said: I said, ""No."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, say: 'Whoever loves to maintain ties with his father in his grave, let him maintain ties with his father's brothers after him.' And there was brotherhood and affection between my father 'Umar and your father, so I wanted to maintain that tie."
"405","[AI-translation] From Abu Umamah, from the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, that he said: ""Whoever loves for the sake of Allah, hates for the sake of Allah, gives for the sake of Allah, and withholds for the sake of Allah, has perfected his faith."
"406","[AI-translation] From Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""Whoever is given a gift and is able, let him recompense it; and whoever is not able, let him praise. For whoever praises has shown gratitude, and whoever conceals it has been ungrateful. And whoever feigns what he was not given is like the wearer of two garments of falsehood."
"407","[AI-translation] Mustawrid that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: ""Whoever eats a meal at the expense of a Muslim man, Allah will feed him the like of it from Hellfire. Whoever clothes himself at the expense of a Muslim man with a garment, Allah will clothe him with the like of it in Hellfire. And whoever stands against a Muslim man in a position of notoriety, Allah will cause him to stand in a position of notoriety on the Day of Resurrection."
"408","[AI-translation] From Mu'awiya ibn Qura, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever eats of these two foul trees, let him not approach our mosque. If you must eat them, then kill them by cooking."
"409","[AI-translation] From Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that he heard a man saying: ""O family of so-and-so!"" He said to him: ""Bite your father's penis,"" and he was not. He said to him: ""O Abu Al-Mundhir, you were not foul-mouthed."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: 'Whoever boasts with the boast of the pre-Islamic period of ignorance, bite his father's penis and do not conceal it.'"
"410","[AI-translation] Yaman, marfu'an: ""Whoever spits facing the Qibla will come on the Day of Resurrection, and his spit will be between his eyes."
"411","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), who said: ""Whoever mentions a man with what is in him has backbitten him, and whoever mentions him with other than what is in him has slandered him."
"412","[AI-translation] From Abu Umamah, marfu'an: ""Whoever shows mercy, even to the slaughter of a sparrow, Allah will have mercy on him on the Day of Resurrection."
"413","[AI-translation] From 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever remains silent is saved."
"414","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""From the fitrah of Islam is: bathing on Friday, using the siwak, trimming the mustache, and letting the beard grow. Indeed, the Magians let their mustaches grow and shave their beards, so oppose them: trim your mustaches and let your beards grow."
"415","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), who said: ""Whoever says when he takes to his bed: 'There is no god but Allah, alone, without partner. His is the dominion and His is the praise, and He is over all things competent. There is no power and no strength except by Allah. Glory be to Allah, and praise be to Allah, and there is no god but Allah, and Allah is the Greatest,' his sins will be forgiven—or he said: his faults, Mis'ar was in doubt—even if they are like the foam of the sea."
"416","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, marfu'an: ""Whoever severs ties of kinship or swears a false oath will see its consequences before he dies."
"417","[AI-translation] Bahili, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not wear silk or gold."
"418","[AI-translation] Narrated Anas bin Malik, raised to the Prophet: ""Whoever restrains his anger, Allah will restrain His punishment from him; whoever guards his tongue, Allah will conceal his shame; and whoever apologizes to Allah, Allah will accept his apology."
"419","[AI-translation] Narrated Jarir who said: The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said: ""Whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy, whoever does not forgive will not be forgiven, and whoever does not repent will not be granted repentance."
"420","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Dharr, raised to the Prophet: ""Your servants who are agreeable to you, feed them from what you eat and clothe them from what you wear. Those who are not agreeable to you, sell them, and do not torment the creation of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"421","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said: ""Whoever Allah protects from the evil of what is between his jaws and the evil of what is between his legs will enter Paradise."
"422","[AI-translation] Narrated Jabir bin Abdullah, raised to the Prophet: ""Whoever is occupied with the need of his brother, Allah will be occupied with his need."
"423","[AI-translation] Narrated Ibn Umar, raised to the Prophet: ""The believer who mixes with people and endures their harm is better than the one who does not mix with people and does not endure their harm."
"424","[AI-translation] Narrated Sahl bin Sa'd, raised to the Prophet: ""The believer is sociable, and there is no good in one who neither befriends nor is befriended."
"425","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, raised to the Prophet: ""The believer is sociable and is befriended, and there is no good in one who is neither sociable nor befriended. The best of people are those most beneficial to people."
"426","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Barzah who said: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, guide me to a deed by which I may benefit."" He said: ""Remove harm from the path of the Muslims."
"427","[AI-translation] Musayyab who said: While the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, was sitting with his companions, a man insulted Abu Bakr and harmed him. Abu Bakr remained silent. Then he insulted him a second time, and Abu Bakr remained silent. Then he insulted him a third time, and Abu Bakr defended himself. When Abu Bakr defended himself, the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, got up. Abu Bakr said: ""Are you angry with me, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""An angel descended from the heaven refuting what he said to you, but when you defended yourself, the devil appeared, and I was not about to sit while the devil was present."
"428","[AI-translation] 'As, elevated [to the Prophet]: He forbade that a man sit between two men except with their permission."
"429","[AI-translation] Narrated from a man among the Companions of the Prophet that the Prophet (peace be upon him) forbade sitting between the sun and the shade, and said: ""It is the sitting place of the Devil."
"430","[AI-translation] Narrated from Jabir who said: He forbade that a man place (in a narration: raise) one of his legs over the other. It was added in the other narration: while lying on his back."
"431","[AI-translation] Narrated from Jabir b. 'Abd Allah who stated that the Prophet (peace be upon him) forbade images in the house and forbade a man from making that."
"432","[AI-translation] Narrated from Ibn 'Umar: He (peace be upon him and his family) forbade solitude: that a man sleep alone or travel alone."
"433","[AI-translation] Narrated from Shaqiq who said: I and a companion of mine entered upon Salman, may Allah be pleased with him. He served us bread and salt and said: ""Were it not that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) forbade us from going to excess, I would have done so for you."" My companion said: ""If only there were sa'tar in our salt."" So he sent his wudu' vessel to the greengrocer and pawned it, and brought sa'tar and put it in it. When we had eaten, my companion said: ""Praise be to Allah who has made us content with what He has provided us."" Salman said: ""Had you been content with what you were provided, my wudu' vessel would not be pawned with the greengrocer."
"434","[AI-translation] Narrated from Mu'awiya b. Qurra from his father who said: A man said: ""O Messenger of Allah! I slaughter the sheep and show mercy to it."" He said: ""And the sheep; if you show it mercy, Allah will show you mercy."
"435","[AI-translation] Narrated from Anas b. Malik, elevated [to the Prophet]: ""By Him in Whose hand is my soul, Allah does not place His mercy except upon the merciful."" They said: ""All of us show mercy."" He said: ""It is not the mercy of one of you for his companion, but rather showing mercy to all people."
"436","[AI-translation] Narrated from Anas b. Malik who said: I used to serve the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), and I used to enter upon him without permission. I came one day and entered upon him, so he said: ""Step back, my son! A matter has occurred, so do not enter upon me except with permission."
"437","[AI-translation] Darda', who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Do not eat reclining, nor on a sieve, and do not take a spot in the mosque where you pray only in it, and do not step over the necks of people on Friday, lest Allah make you a bridge for them on the Day of Resurrection."
"438","[AI-translation] On the authority of Jabir, marfu': ""Do not give permission to one who does not begin with salam."
"439","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurayrah, marfu': Do not initiate salam with the Jews and the Christians, and when you meet any of them on a road, force them to its narrowest part."
"440","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurayrah, marfu': Do not combine my name and my kunyah (I am Abu al-Qasim, Allah gives and I apportion)."
"441","On the authority of Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, who said: I entered upon Zaynab bint Abi Salamah and asked her about the name of a sister of hers who was with her. He said: I said, ""Her name is Barrah."" She said, ""Change her name, for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Zaynab bint Jahsh, and her name was Barrah, so he changed her name to Zaynab."" Then he entered upon Umm Salamah when he married her, and my name was Barrah. He heard them calling me Barrah, and he said: ""Do not praise yourselves (tazakku), for Allah knows best the righteous (al-barrah) from among you and the sinful (al-fajirah). Name her Zaynab."" Umm Salamah said: ""Then she is Zaynab."" I said to her: ""What about my name?"" She said: ""Change it to what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed it to; name her Zaynab."""
"442","On the authority of Abū Jurayy Jābir ibn Sulaym, who said: I saw a man whom the people were following and acting upon his opinion; he did not say anything except that they acted upon it. I asked: “Who is this?” They said: “This is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” I said: “Upon you be peace, O Messenger of Allah,” twice. He said: “Do not say ‘Upon you be peace,’ for ‘Upon you be peace’ is the greeting for the dead. Say: ‘Peace be upon you.’” I said: “Are you the Messenger of Allah?” He said: “I am the Messenger of Allah, the One whom, when harm afflicts you and you call upon Him, He removes it from you; and when a year of drought afflicts you and you call upon Him, He causes crops to grow for you; and when you are in a barren land or a desert and your mount strays and you call upon Him, He returns it to you.” I said: “Give me some counsel.” He said: “Do not revile anyone.” He said: “And do not belittle any good deed, even that you meet your brother with a cheerful face, for that is part of goodness. Raise your lower garment to mid-calf; if you refuse, then to the ankles. Beware of trailing the lower garment, for it is from arrogance, and Allah does not love arrogance. And if a man insults you and reproaches you with something he knows about you, do not reproach him with what you know about him, for the burden of that is upon him.” And he added after his saying “Do not revile anyone”: He said: “So I never reviled anyone after that—neither a free man, nor a slave, nor a camel, nor a sheep.”"
"443","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, that he used to say: Do not relate a dream except to a scholar or a sincere adviser."
"444","[AI-translation] The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: The gecko is a little miscreant. It is reported from the hadith of Aisha and Sa'd bin Abu Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"445","[AI-translation] On the authority of 'Abdullah bin Buraydah, from his father, marfu': Do not say to the hypocrite, ""Our master,"" for if he is your master, you have angered your Lord, Mighty and Majestic."
"446","[AI-translation] Narrated Ibn Abbas: A man's cloak was snatched by the wind during the time of the Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, so he cursed it. The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: Do not curse the wind, for it is commanded. And whoever curses something that does not deserve it, the curse returns upon him."
"447","[AI-translation] Narrated Ibn Abbas: A man's cloak was snatched by the wind during the time of the Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, so he cursed it. The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: Do not curse the wind, for it is commanded. And whoever curses something that does not deserve it, the curse returns upon him."
"448","[AI-translation] On the authority of Jabir, marfu': Do not sit on the main roads, and do not fulfill needs upon them."
"449","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurayrah: It was said to the Prophet, peace be upon him: O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so stands the night in prayer, fasts the day, and does [good], gives charity, and harms her neighbors with her tongue. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: There is no good in her; she is of the people of the Fire. He said: And so-and-so prays the prescribed prayers, gives charity from portions (of dried cheese), and does not harm anyone. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""She is of the people of Paradise."
"450","[AI-translation] From 'Abdullah, marfu'an: No night talk except for one praying or traveling."
"451","[AI-translation] From Salman, from the Prophet, peace be upon him and his family, who said: ""Let no one overburden himself for his guest with what he cannot afford."
"452","[AI-translation] From Sahl bin Sa'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, said: ""A man should not sit between a man and his son in the gathering."
"453","[AI-translation] From Hisham bin 'Amir, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, say: ""It is not lawful for a Muslim to boycott a Muslim for more than three nights. Both are deviating from the truth as long as they remain in their estrangement. The first of them to return, his precedence in returning is his expiation. If he greets and his greeting is not returned, the angels return it to him, and the devil returns it to the other. If they die while maintaining their severance, they will never be gathered together in Paradise."
"454","[AI-translation] Yaman, marfu'an: A talebearer will not enter Paradise."
"455","[AI-translation] Ash'ath bin Qays, marfu'an: He who does not thank people does not thank Allah."
"456","[AI-translation] Samit, marfu'an: Let none of you bite another."
"457","[AI-translation] From Muhammad bin Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him and his family, said: ""Let none of you say: 'I planted,' but let him say: 'I sowed.'"" Muhammad said: Abu Hurayrah said: ""Have you not heard the statement of Allah, Blessed and Exalted: 'Do you see what you sow? Is it you who make it grow, or are We the grower?' (Al-Waqi'ah)"
"458","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, marfu'an: ""Let none of you say: 'My slave,' for all of you are slaves of Allah. But let him say: 'My boy.' And let the slave not say: 'My lord,' but let him say: 'My master.'"
"459","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, marfu'an: ""A man should not stand up for a man from his seat, but rather make space, and Allah will make space for you."
"460","[AI-translation] From Jabir, marfu': Let none of you make his brother get up on Friday, then go to his seat and sit in it, but let him say: ""Make room."
"461","[AI-translation] From Zurarah bin Awfa: 'Abdullah bin Salam narrated to me, saying: When the Prophet arrived in Madinah, the people rushed towards him, and it was said: ""The Messenger of Allah has arrived! The Messenger of Allah has arrived! The Messenger of Allah has arrived!"" three times. So I came among the people to look, and when his face became clear to me, I recognized that his face was not the face of a liar. The first thing I heard him say was: ""O people! Spread the salam, feed the food, maintain ties of kinship, and pray at night while the people are asleep, [and] you will enter Paradise in peace."
"462","[AI-translation] From Ibn Umar: That the Messenger of Allah returned from a campaign and said: ""O people, do not visit women by night, nor take them unawares."
"463","[AI-translation] From Aishah, she said: A man sought permission to enter upon the Messenger of Allah while I was with him. He said: ""What an evil son of the tribe,"" or ""brother of the tribe."" Then he granted him permission and spoke gently to him. When he left, I said: ""O Messenger of Allah! You said what you said, then you spoke gently to him?"" He said: ""O Aishah! The worst of people are those whom people leave or abandon out of fear of their foulness."
"464","Al-Silsila-tu-Ahadees-e-Sahiha Hadees: 464 Arabic Hadees:"
"465","Furwa ibn Mujahid al-Lakhmi narrated from Uqba ibn Amir who said: I met the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and he said to me: ""O Uqba ibn Amir! Maintain ties with the one who severs ties with you, give to the one who deprives you, and pardon the one who wrongs you."" He said: Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and he said to me: ""O Uqba ibn Amir! Restrain your tongue, let your house suffice you, and weep over your sin."" Then I met the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and he said to me: ""O Uqba ibn Amir! Shall I not teach you surahs the like of which have not been revealed in the Torah, nor in the Psalms, nor in the Gospel, nor in the Criterion (Quran)? Let no night come upon you except that you recite them in it: 'Say: He is Allah, the One' (Surah al-Ikhlas), 'Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of the daybreak' (Surah al-Falaq), 'Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind' (Surah an-Nas)."" Uqba said: So no night came upon me thereafter except that I recited them in it, and it is incumbent upon me not to abandon them, for the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) commanded me to do so. And whenever Furwa ibn Mujahid narrated this hadith, he would say: ""So woe to the one who does not restrain his tongue, or does not weep over his sin, or whose house does not suffice him."""
"466","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, mawquf: One of you sees the speck in his brother's eye, but forgets the trunk—or the beam—traversing his own eye."
"467","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah said: ""A neck will emerge from the Fire on the Day of Resurrection, having two eyes that see, two ears that hear, and a tongue that speaks, saying: 'I have been entrusted with three: every obstinate tyrant, everyone who called upon another god alongside Allah, and the image-makers.'"
"468","[AI-translation] From Zayd bin Aslam, that the Messenger of Allah said: ""The rider gives salam to the walker, and if one of the people gives salam, it suffices on their behalf."
"469","[AI-translation] Rahman bin Shibl, he said: I heard the Prophet say: ""The rider gives salam to the walker, the walker gives salam to the sitting, and the fewer give salam to the greater. Whoever answers the salam, it is for him, and whoever does not answer, has nothing."
"470","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah, marfu': ""The rider gives salam to the walker, the walker gives salam to the sitting, and the few give salam to the many."
"471","[AI-translation] From Jabir, mawquf: ""The rider gives salam to the walker, the walker gives salam to the sitting, and between two walkers, whichever of them begins with the salam is better."
"472","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurairah, marfu'an: The young greets the old, the passerby greets the seated, and the few greet the many."
"473","[AI-translation] Narrated Fadalah bin Ubaid, marfu'an: The rider greets the walker, the walker greets the seated, and the few greet the many."
"474","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurairah, marfu'an: Allah laughs at two men, one of whom kills the other, yet both are in Paradise. One fights in the path of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and is martyred. Then Allah accepts the repentance of the killer, he embraces Islam, fights in the path of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and is martyred."
